ssion which is  a famous ground from the Baroque period  called la folia  [Music]  now let's hear the difference in the  context of a piece this is a really nice  Chaconne by D visa  [Music]  so my first reaction is that I'm totally  blown away by these strings I think I  didn't expect to like them so much  there's a warmth and resonance to the  gut strings that synthetic strings in my  opinion just don't have especially when  you play close to the bridge the gut  strings still are resonant whereas the  synthetic strings can just sound sort of  plasticky another big difference I'm  noticing is that the long bass strings  don't sustain as long they they're  wonderfully loud and resonant but the  decay is quicker which actually I prefer  because in solo music often you're  playing scaler bass notes and if they  sustain for too long they overlap and it  can get a bit muddy  [Music]  so I'm just having so much fun getting  used to these and hearing how they  change the quality of this instrument  which I know so well they're still quite  new but I'm already kind of sensing some  serious pros and cons in terms of the  pros I think they just sound much better  there's this warmth and resonance that I  think you can't get with synthetic  strings another one is that they're  historical and there's something really  cool about knowing that I'm playing on  the authentic strings that someone 400  years ago would have used and lastly I  love that there's less sustain so that  in general I can work less and it sounds  cleaner actually in terms of the cons  the first thing is that these are  actually really expensive just this low  bass string was like $40 and so it's a  bit of an investment also because their  gut strings they react more harshly to  humidity changes in the air which means  you just have to accept that you'll tune  more often and lastly I don't know this  from first-hand experience yet because  these are new but I know for my  colleagues that especially with thes